WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:04.050
Picture a team meeting. A newer colleague raises a concern

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.530
about a plan to change how reviews are scheduled. She

3
00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.230
points out, carefully, that the change could slow things down

4
00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.670
for people who rely on interpreters. The room goes quiet.

5
00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.750
Everyone is watching the leader, and everyone is waiting to

6
00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.450
see what happens to someone who disagrees.

7
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:21.700
That silence says a lot, doesn't it? People in that

8
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.500
room already know something about how this leader responds. So

9
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.020
tell me, what are we actually talking about today when

10
00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:28.700
we use the phrase authoritarian leadership?

11
00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:34.110
Authoritarian leadership is authority held for its own sake. Decisions,

12
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.810
information and control all sit with one person, and obedience

13
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:40.870
is treated as a sign of loyalty and competence. The

14
00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.790
important word is purpose. Here, compliance is not only a

15
00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:45.710
way to get work done. It becomes the goal.

16
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.540
I want to be careful here, because plenty of good

17
00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:53.400
leaders are firm and decisive. Giving clear orders is sometimes

18
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.660
exactly what a team needs. How is that different from

19
00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:57.360
what you are describing?

20
00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.630
That is the right question, and the page draws the

21
00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:04.390
line clearly. Directive leadership gives firm direction because speed or

22
00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:08.230
precision really matters, and then it steps back. Authoritarian leadership

23
00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:13.090
is a power-preserving posture. Questions are heard as challenges. Disagreement

24
00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:16.570
is treated as disloyalty. The firmness is about the leader,

25
00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:17.450
not the work.

26
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.340
So if I worked for someone like that, what would

27
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.140
I actually notice from day to day? I imagine it

28
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.640
isn't always shouting or dramatic moments in meetings.

29
00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:30.410
Often it is quieter than that. Expectations change without explanation.

30
00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:33.630
Information is rationed, so people depend on the leader for

31
00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:37.170
every piece of context. Mistakes are handled through reprimands or

32
00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.130
being left out of meetings, rather than through coaching. Good

33
00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:42.530
assignments tend to go to people who agree, not necessarily

34
00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:43.870
to people with the strongest judgment.

35
00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:48.570
And conversations between teams are discouraged unless they go through

36
00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:48.890
the top.

37
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.600
I suspect some listeners will recognize a little of themselves

38
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.440
in that list, especially on a hard week. Is that

39
00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.700
something people should feel ashamed of, or is there a

40
00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.060
more useful way to think about it?

41
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:02.450
There is a more useful way. These are patterns, and

42
00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.830
patterns often show up under pressure: a deadline, a crisis,

43
00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:08.690
a leader who was managed this way and learned it

44
00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:11.870
as normal. Recognizing the pattern is the first step out

45
00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.910
of it. This page describes an approach, never a person,

46
00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:16.590
and every approach can change.

47
00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:20.180
To be fair to the style, is there ever a

48
00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:23.000
moment when that kind of tight control actually helps an

49
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.100
organization, even briefly?

50
00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:28.890
The page is honest about this. In rare situations, such

51
00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:32.350
as an immediate threat to physical safety or serious misconduct

52
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:35.950
where negotiation is impossible, one clear line of authority can

53
00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.110
force fast compliance. Nobody wonders who is in charge.

54
00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:43.150
But those are exceptions, and the healthier tool for them

55
00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:47.010
is directive leadership, which explains its reasons and hands control

56
00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.570
back when the danger has passed.

57
00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.740
So what happens over time, when this becomes the normal

58
00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.120
way a team is led? What does it do to

59
00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:56.600
the work and to the people doing it?

60
00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:00.870
The costs build slowly. People do exactly what they are

61
00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.470
told and nothing more. Problem solving stops whenever the leader

62
00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:07.610
is away. Most seriously, the leader loses touch with reality,

63
00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:10.750
because staff learn that bad news is dangerous to deliver.

64
00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.910
Small problems grow into large ones before anyone at the

65
00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:14.770
top hears about them.

66
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:18.550
And the strongest, most principled people often leave for healthier

67
00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:19.490
places to work.

68
00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:24.720
This program connects every leadership style to fair service and

69
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.980
to the experience of staff. Where does authoritarian leadership land

70
00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:28.600
on that question?

71
00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.470
It works against equity, even when the leader says they

72
00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:35.790
support it. Inclusion depends on people feeling safe to raise

73
00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:40.370
concerns about bias, inaccessible tools or unfair norms. In a

74
00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:44.090
fear-based culture, staff who already face extra scrutiny, including people

75
00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.690
of color, disabled staff and people whose communication styles differ

76
00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:50.250
from the leader's, will reasonably choose silence.

77
00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:55.670
One person's unexamined assumptions also become policy, because nobody can

78
00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:56.330
question them.

79
00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.760
And I imagine the effect does not stay inside the

80
00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.900
office. People who are treated rigidly at work may carry

81
00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.740
that rigidity into how they treat the public.

82
00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:10.070
Exactly. Front line staff who are denied any discretion often

83
00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:13.610
pass that rigidity on, enforcing rules without room for the

84
00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:17.010
real barriers people face. Communities that already have reasons to

85
00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:20.630
distrust government notice it quickly. Even equity initiatives become a

86
00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:23.710
box to check to avoid trouble, and they fade as

87
00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:25.310
soon as the leader's attention moves on.

88
00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.160
Let's go back to that meeting with the newer colleague

89
00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:31.280
and the interpreter concern. What would a different response have

90
00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:32.300
looked like in that moment?

91
00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:35.870
Thanking her for raising it, asking what she has seen,

92
00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:38.770
and agreeing to check the effect on interpreter requests before

93
00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.850
the change goes ahead. The leader keeps their authority and

94
00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:45.010
uses it well. The whole room learns that candor is

95
00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:47.490
welcome, and the delay is caught before it reaches the

96
00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:48.450
people it would have affected.

97
00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.500
For anyone listening who wants to move away from this

98
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.760
pattern, or who supervises someone who leads this way, what

99
00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.040
are some practical first steps?

100
00:04:56.770 --> 00:05:01.310
Start small and visible. Explain the reasons behind decisions. When

101
00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:04.630
someone disagrees, thank them before you respond, and ask one

102
00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:08.990
question to understand. Share context instead of rationing it. At

103
00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:11.970
the organizational level, the page suggests reporting channels that do

104
00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:15.250
not route through the direct supervisor, feedback from staff included

105
00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:19.030
in how supervisors are evaluated, and documented reasons for decisions

106
00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.910
about hiring, promotion and policy.

107
00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.380
Those sound achievable, which I think matters. Change here does

108
00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:29.400
not require becoming a different person overnight. It starts with

109
00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:30.840
how the next disagreement is handled.

110
00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:33.810
That is a good place to end. Here is a

111
00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:36.550
question to carry with you. When someone disagrees with you

112
00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.250
in front of others, what do you feel first, and

113
00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:41.150
what do you do with that feeling? Thank you for

114
00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:41.450
listening.
